**transparence, -ency.** The second is the usual form. The first is marked *rare* in the OED; & indeed, in its only two *-ence* quotations that are as late as 1800 euphony plainly accounts for the avoidance of *-cy*:—*Motive may be detected through the transparence* of *tendency.*/*Adamantine solidity, transparence, & brilliancy.*
>>>

<<<
transparent, & the synonyms diaphanous, pellucid, translucent. Transparent is the general word for describing what is penetrable by sight (lit. or fig.) or by light, & it can be substituted for any of the others unless there is some point of precision or of rhetoric to be gained.
===
**transparent,** & the synonyms *diaphanous, pellucid, translucent.* *Transparent* is the general word for describing what is penetrable by sight (lit. or fig.) or by light, & it can be substituted for any of the others unless there is some point of precision or of rhetoric to be gained.
>>>

